<?php

/*
 * User Manual, chapter 6: EIR and Revenue Recognition.
 *
 * Every label, column, status and message below is quoted from the Vue pages
 * under resources/js/Pages/Eir, the Eir* controllers and the services under
 * app/Services/Eir. Keep them in step when the screens change.
 */

return [
    'EIR and Revenue Recognition' => [

        'How the EIR module fits together' => [
            'body' => '<p>The effective interest rate (EIR) is the rate that exactly discounts a loan\'s contractual cash flows, including the fees that are part of making the loan, back to the amount MAIIC actually advanced. IFRS 9 requires interest income to be recognised at that rate rather than at the rate printed on the offer letter. The EIR module is the set of screens that gets the platform from raw bank extracts to a locked EIR for every loan, a monthly interest figure built from it, and an explanation of how that figure differs from what the general ledger (GL) posted. Open the sidebar and expand <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>: the six entries are <b>Accounting Rules</b>, <b>EIR Data</b>, <b>Fee Classification</b>, <b>EIR Calculations</b>, <b>GL Reconciliation</b> and <b>Coverage &amp; Blockers</b>. The intake screen, <b>EIR Data Intake</b>, is reached from the button of that name at the top right of EIR Data. All of these screens require the Settings permission.</p><h4>The pipeline, in order</h4><ol><li><b>Accounting rules.</b> A small rulebook says which kinds of fee are integral to the EIR (part of the loan\'s yield) and which are not. Rules only suggest; they never decide.</li><li><b>Intake.</b> Extracts A, B and C, fee files and repayment schedules are uploaded through EIR Data Intake. Every import runs in the background and reports contract by contract.</li><li><b>Fee classification.</b> Each imported fee line is classified integral or non-integral with a written reason, then independently reviewed by a second person.</li><li><b>Schedule approval.</b> The original repayment schedule (version 1) is generated from Extract A terms, compared with the remaining schedule in Extract B, and approved on the Schedule Review tab of EIR Data.</li><li><b>Readiness.</b> Before a contract can be solved the platform checks its terms, its schedule and its fees. A contract is READY or BLOCKED, and each blocker has a code and a plain-English message.</li><li><b>Calculation and lock.</b> EIR Calculations solves the rate, a second person approves it, and the rate is locked. A locked rate is permanent unless an administrator reopens it with a reason.</li><li><b>Monthly revenue run.</b> A command run by the administrator rolls each locked loan forward one month: opening balance, interest at the EIR, cash received, closing balance.</li><li><b>GL reconciliation.</b> The calculated interest is set against what the ledger posted (Extract C) and the difference is split into named effects.</li><li><b>Coverage.</b> Coverage &amp; Blockers shows how much of the book is locked, by count and by exposure, and ranks whatever is holding back the rest.</li></ol><h4>The three doors: Extracts A, B and C</h4><p><b>Extract A, the contract master.</b> One row per facility with its terms: origination date, maturity, drawn amount, contractual rate, repayment frequency, moratorium, arrangement and legal fees. It creates the EIR contract profile. Without it a loan shows the blocker CONTRACT_PROFILE_MISSING and nothing else can happen.</p><p><b>Extract B, contract transactions.</b> Every scheduled and actual cash movement per loan. Rows flagged Scheduled become the remaining schedule used only as comparison evidence; rows flagged Actual are kept as transaction evidence and feed the cash received in the monthly revenue run. Extract B never replaces the version-1 schedule.</p><p><b>Extract C, GL interest postings.</b> Interest income posted to the ledger per loan per month. It is the other side of the reconciliation and is never used to calculate anything.</p><h4>Who does what</h4><ul><li><b>Finance officer (maker):</b> loads extracts, classifies fees, generates draft schedules and requests calculations.</li><li><b>Reviewer (checker):</b> approves rules, reviews fee classifications, approves version-1 schedules and approves and locks EIRs. The checker must be a different person from the maker.</li><li><b>Administrator:</b> can override maker and checker where the screen says so, reopens locked EIRs, and runs the monthly revenue command.</li></ul><h4>Maker and checker in plain words</h4><p>A maker is the person who does a piece of work; a checker is the second person who confirms it. The platform enforces the split where it matters most: the message "A classifier cannot review their own decision." stops you approving fee lines you classified, and "The calculator cannot approve and lock their own EIR." stops you locking a rate you calculated. Administrators can override both, and every override is written to the audit log with a flag so an auditor can see it.</p><h4>What happens next</h4><p>Once a loan is locked, its EIR feeds two things: the monthly revenue run, which produces the interest figure reconciled against the GL, and the ECL engine, which discounts expected losses at the locked EIR. Reopening a locked EIR therefore invalidates both, which is why it needs a reason and an administrator.</p>',
            'images' => ['eir-data' => 'EIR Data, the hub screen of the EIR module, with its four tabs'],
        ],

        'Accounting rules' => [
            'body' => '<p>Accounting Rules is the rulebook that suggests whether an imported fee or cost is integral to the EIR. An integral fee is one that is part of making the loan (an arrangement fee, a direct legal cost) and so belongs in the yield; a non-integral fee is charged for something else (a monitoring service, a penalty) and stays out. Rules make suggestions only: the green notice at the top reads "Rules make suggestions only. Every imported line remains pending until classified and independently reviewed." Finance sets rules up; a reviewer approves them. Open the sidebar, expand <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b> and choose <b>Accounting Rules</b>.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li>Page title <b>EIR Accounting Rules</b> with the strapline "Controlled defaults that suggest whether imported fees and costs are integral to EIR".</li><li>The green notice quoted above.</li><li>A card headed <b>Rule register</b> with the <b>Create rule</b> button at its right.</li><li>Table columns, in order: <b>Priority / rule</b> (priority number, a dot, the rule name, and an <b>Active</b> or <b>Inactive</b> badge), <b>Matching criteria</b> (three lines: "Type:", "Description:", "Direction:" and "GL:", each showing <code>any</code> or <code>either</code> when blank), <b>Treatment</b> (an <b>Integral</b> or <b>Non-integral</b> badge with the rationale beneath), <b>Approval</b> (<b>Approved</b> in green with the approver\'s name, or <b>Pending approval</b> in amber) and <b>Actions</b>.</li><li>Action links per row: <b>Edit</b>, <b>Approve</b> (shown only while the rule is pending), and <b>Disable</b> or <b>Enable</b> depending on the current state.</li><li>Empty state: "No rules created yet."</li><li>The rule modal, titled <b>Create accounting rule</b> or <b>Edit accounting rule</b>, with the note "Define the conditions and suggested IFRS 9 treatment." and two sections: <b>Rule identity</b> and <b>Matching conditions</b> ("Blank conditions match any value."). Footer buttons <b>Cancel</b> and <b>Create rule</b> or <b>Update rule</b>; the button reads "Saving…" while it works.</li></ul><h4>Field by field</h4><ul><li><b>Rule name</b> (required, up to 255 characters). Placeholder "e.g. Direct arrangement fees".</li><li><b>Priority</b> (required, whole number from 1 to 65535, default 100). Lower numbers are tried first.</li><li><b>Fee/cost type</b>: <code>Any type</code>, <code>arrangement</code>, <code>legal</code>, <code>appraisal</code>, <code>default</code>, <code>levy</code> or <code>other</code>. Matched ignoring capitals.</li><li><b>Description contains</b>: free text that must appear somewhere in the fee description, ignoring capitals.</li><li><b>GL account</b>: must match the fee line\'s GL reference exactly.</li><li><b>Cash direction</b>: <code>Either</code>, <code>Received by MAIIC</code> or <code>Paid by MAIIC</code>.</li><li><b>Proposed treatment</b>: <code>Integral - include in EIR</code> or <code>Non-integral - exclude from EIR</code>.</li><li><b>Accounting rationale</b> (required, up to 2000 characters): why this treatment is right. It is shown in the register and copied into the classification reason when the rule is applied.</li><li><b>Make this rule active</b> checkbox.</li></ul><h4>How a rule is matched</h4><p>Only rules that are both <b>Active</b> and <b>Approved</b> take part. The platform walks them in priority order (then by age) and uses the first rule where every filled-in condition matches the fee line. A rule with all conditions blank matches everything, so give it a high priority number so it acts as the catch-all.</p><h4>To approve a rule</h4><ol><li>Read the matching criteria and the rationale in the register.</li><li>Press <b>Approve</b> on the row. The badge changes to <b>Approved</b> with your name; the message reads "Accounting rule approved."</li><li>Go to Fee Classification and press <b>Re-apply rules to pending lines</b> so lines already on file pick up the new suggestion.</li></ol><p>The register records who approved but does not itself stop the author of a rule pressing Approve; MAIIC\'s control policy should require a second person.</p><h4>To disable or re-enable a rule</h4><ol><li>Press <b>Disable</b>; the badge becomes <b>Inactive</b> and the message reads "Rule deactivated." The rule is kept for history but no longer matches.</li><li>Press <b>Enable</b> to bring it back ("Rule activated."). Approval is not affected.</li></ol><h4>What happens next</h4><p>New fee lines imported after approval carry the rule\'s name and treatment in the <b>Rule suggestion</b> column of Fee Classification. Lines that were already pending are only updated when someone presses <b>Re-apply rules to pending lines</b>. Lines already classified or reviewed are never changed by a rule. Every create, update, approve and toggle is written to the audit log.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"Rule updated; approval has been reset."</b> Editing any field clears the approval. A reviewer must approve the rule again before it matches anything.</li><li><b>"Accounting rule created and awaiting approval."</b> This is normal after Create rule; the rule is inert until approved.</li><li><b>A rule is approved but lines show "No matching rule".</b> Either the rule is Inactive, a condition does not match (a GL account must match exactly), or the lines were imported before approval: press Re-apply rules to pending lines.</li><li><b>A red message appears in the modal.</b> The first validation error is shown; the usual causes are a blank name, a blank rationale or a priority below 1.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open the sidebar, expand EIR & Revenue Recognition and choose Accounting Rules.',
                'Press Create rule.',
                'Enter the Rule name and a Priority (lower numbers are tried first).',
                'Set the matching conditions: Fee/cost type, Description contains, GL account and Cash direction. Leave a condition blank to match any value.',
                'Choose the Proposed treatment: Integral - include in EIR, or Non-integral - exclude from EIR.',
                'Write the Accounting rationale and tick Make this rule active.',
                'Press Create rule. The message reads "Accounting rule created and awaiting approval."',
                'Ask a reviewer to press Approve on the row, then press Re-apply rules to pending lines on Fee Classification.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-rules' => 'The EIR Accounting Rules register with priority, criteria, treatment and approval'],
            'routes' => ['eir-accounting-rules.index', 'eir-accounting-rules.store', 'eir-accounting-rules.update', 'eir-accounting-rules.approve', 'eir-accounting-rules.toggle'],
        ],

        'EIR data' => [
            'body' => '<p>EIR Data is the hub of the module: it shows what has been loaded, lets you generate and approve each loan\'s original repayment schedule, and gives a first view of the GL reconciliation. Finance officers use the first three tabs day to day; reviewers use the Schedule Review tab to approve. Open the sidebar, expand <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b> and choose <b>EIR Data</b>.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li>Breadcrumb "EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition / EIR Data", title <b>EIR Data</b>, strapline "Review source records, calculated interest, and the GL reconciliation".</li><li>Header buttons <b>EIR Data Intake</b> (opens the import screen) and <b>Calculate EIR</b> (opens EIR Calculations).</li><li>Four KPI tiles: <b>Contracts</b>, <b>Cash-flow rows</b>, <b>GL postings</b> and <b>Locked EIRs</b>.</li><li>Four tabs, each with a count: <b>Contract Master</b>, <b>Cash Flows</b>, <b>Schedule Review</b> and <b>GL Reconciliation</b>. A one-line description sits under the active tab\'s name.</li><li>A search box with placeholder "Contract, account or reference" and a <b>Search</b> button. On Schedule Review there is also a comparison dropdown (<code>All comparison results</code>, <code>Within tolerance</code>, <code>Principal variance</code>, <code>Interest variance</code>, <code>No Extract B evidence</code>, <code>Not compared / blocked</code>, each with its count) and a <b>Clear</b> button once a filter is set.</li><li>On Schedule Review, an amber bar with <b>Dry-run readiness</b>, <b>Generate eligible drafts</b> and the note "Extract B remaining rows are comparison evidence. Only an approved version-1 schedule is used by EIR."</li><li>On GL Reconciliation, six cards: <b>Calculated rows</b>, <b>Within tolerance</b>, <b>Exceptions</b>, <b>Matched GL interest</b>, <b>Calculated EIR interest</b> and <b>Net EIR &minus; GL</b>, with the note that variance is calculated EIR interest minus GL interest and tolerance is plus or minus 1% of the GL amount.</li><li>Page links (<b>Previous</b>, page numbers, <b>Next</b>) under every table.</li></ul><h4>The four tables</h4><ul><li><b>Contract Master</b> columns: <b>Contract</b> (with sub-account or GL code), <b>Portfolio / product</b>, <b>Terms</b> (origination to maturity, frequency and rate), <b>Amounts</b> (drawn amount and currency, approved amount), <b>Source coverage</b> (number of cash flows and fees, source system) and <b>EIR status</b> (PENDING, CALCULATED, BLOCKED or LOCKED, with the effective annual rate once solved). Frequency reads Annual, Semi-annual, Quarterly, Bi-monthly, Monthly or "Frequency pending". Empty state: "No records found. Use EIR Data Intake to load data."</li><li><b>Cash Flows</b> columns: <b>Contract</b>, <b>Version</b>, <b>Due date</b>, <b>Principal</b>, <b>Interest</b>, <b>Fees</b>, <b>Total due</b>, <b>Source</b>.</li><li><b>Schedule Review</b> columns: <b>Contract</b>, <b>Extract A terms</b> (frequency, rate, dates, and any readiness issue in red), <b>Generated</b> (rows in version 1), <b>Extract B remaining</b> (rows and the cutoff date), <b>Comparison</b> (badge plus principal and interest differences), <b>Approval</b> and <b>Actions</b> with <b>View schedule</b>, <b>Generate draft</b> (greyed out until the contract is ready, hidden once approved) and <b>Approve v1</b> (only while DRAFT).</li><li><b>GL Reconciliation</b> columns: <b>Contract</b>, <b>Period</b>, <b>GL account</b>, <b>GL interest</b>, <b>EIR interest</b>, <b>EIR &minus; GL</b>, <b>Variance %</b>, <b>Status</b> and <b>Source</b>. Empty state: "No GL postings match this search."</li></ul><h4>Statuses on this screen</h4><ul><li>Comparison: <b>WITHIN TOLERANCE</b> (generated and Extract B agree within 1% or MWK 1 on both principal and interest), <b>PRINCIPAL VARIANCE</b>, <b>INTEREST VARIANCE</b>, <b>NO REMAINING DATA</b> (no Extract B rows for this loan) and <b>NOT GENERATED</b> (no comparison yet).</li><li>Approval: <b>NOT GENERATED</b>, <b>DRAFT</b>, <b>PENDING REVIEW</b> and <b>APPROVED</b>.</li><li>Reconciliation: <b>Within tolerance</b>, <b>Variance</b> and <b>Not calculated</b> (no revenue row exists for that loan and month yet).</li></ul><h4>How a draft schedule is generated</h4><p>The platform builds equal instalments from Extract A: drawn amount, contractual rate, payments per year, origination and maturity dates and moratorium months. The first due date is the later of the stated first repayment date and origination plus moratorium plus one payment interval. The rows are written as version 1 with source GENERATED and the approval status becomes DRAFT. The comparison then takes every Extract B remaining row, uses its earliest due date as the cutoff, and compares totals of principal and interest from that date onwards.</p><h4>To view a schedule</h4><ol><li>On Schedule Review press <b>View schedule</b>. The page <b>Loan Schedule</b> opens with four cards: <b>Approval status</b>, <b>Comparison</b>, <b>Principal variance (MWK)</b> and <b>Interest variance (MWK)</b>.</li><li>Read the two tables, <b>Generated version-1 schedule</b> and <b>Extract B remaining schedule</b>, each with columns <b>#</b>, <b>Due date</b>, <b>Principal (MWK)</b>, <b>Interest (MWK)</b>, <b>Fees (MWK)</b>, <b>Total (MWK)</b> and <b>Source</b>, and a totals row "Total · n rows".</li><li>Press <b>Back to review</b> to return.</li></ol><h4>To generate drafts for the whole book</h4><ol><li>Press <b>Dry-run readiness</b>. The message reads "Schedule dry run: X eligible; Y blocked." Nothing is written.</li><li>Fix blocked contracts through intake, then press <b>Generate eligible drafts</b>. The message reads "Generated N draft schedule(s); M skipped." Approved schedules are never overwritten.</li></ol><h4>What happens next</h4><p>An approved version-1 schedule clears the blocker SCHEDULE_NOT_APPROVED and the contract can be solved on EIR Calculations. The approver, time, comparison status and review note are stored on the contract.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Red text under Extract A terms</b> such as "Origination date is missing", "Maturity date is missing", "Drawn amount is not positive", "Contractual rate is missing or invalid", "Payment frequency is invalid", "Payment frequency was not stated by the source", "Maturity must be after origination", "Equity-excluded instrument" or "EIR is already locked": correct the source and re-import Extract A.</li><li><b>"Approved schedule cannot be overwritten."</b> An approved schedule is final; only an administrator reopening the EIR changes that.</li><li><b>"First repayment date falls after maturity."</b> The terms are inconsistent; check the extract.</li><li><b>"Generate or import an original schedule before approval."</b> There is no version 1 yet.</li><li><b>"Only a draft schedule can be approved."</b> The status is not DRAFT or PENDING REVIEW.</li><li><b>"A review note is required when the generated schedule does not reconcile within tolerance to Extract B remaining cash flows."</b> Type a note explaining the variance in the modal.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR Data and choose the Schedule Review tab.',
                'Find the contract (use the search box or the comparison dropdown) and check the Comparison badge and the principal and interest differences.',
                'Press View schedule if you want to read the generated and Extract B rows side by side, then Back to review.',
                'Press Approve v1. The modal "Approve contractual schedule" opens showing the contract, its comparison badge, Principal variance and Interest variance.',
                'Type a Review note. It is required when the comparison is outside tolerance ("Required because the comparison is outside tolerance.") and optional otherwise.',
                'Press Approve version 1. The message reads "Approved the version-1 schedule for <contract>." and the Approval column shows APPROVED.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-data' => 'EIR Data with the Contract Master, Cash Flows, Schedule Review and GL Reconciliation tabs'],
            'routes' => ['eir-data.index', 'eir-schedules.show', 'eir-schedules.dry-run', 'eir-schedules.generate-all', 'eir-schedules.generate', 'eir-schedules.approve'],
        ],

        'Schedule intake: importing Extracts A, B and C and schedules' => [
            'body' => '<p>EIR Data Intake loads the files the EIR module runs on: the contract master (Extract A), contract transactions (Extract B), GL interest postings (Extract C), fee files and per-contract repayment schedules. Files arrive in whatever column layout the bank system exports; the platform recognises the columns from its built-in aliases and any saved mapping template, validates the required ones, and runs the import in the background so you can watch it complete. Finance officers use it at each month end. Open the sidebar, expand <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b>, choose <b>EIR Data</b> and press <b>EIR Data Intake</b>.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li>Breadcrumb "EIR Data / Data Intake", title <b>EIR Data Intake</b>, strapline "Load contract terms, transactions, fees and loan-level interest evidence", and a green pill at the right reading "Coverage: n/m contracts" (contracts on the latest loan tape that have a schedule).</li><li>Card <b>Import EIR data</b> with the note "Choose the source type and upload the corresponding CSV or Excel file. The system detects and validates the expected columns automatically."</li><li><b>Import type</b> dropdown with five entries: <code>Contract master (Extract A)</code> (facility terms, monthly), <code>Repayment schedule (per contract, once)</code>, <code>Fees and transaction costs</code>, <code>Contract transactions (Extract B)</code> (scheduled and actual cash flows) and <code>GL interest postings (Extract C)</code> (what the ledger posted).</li><li><b>File (CSV / XLSX)</b> with a <b>Choose File</b> button. Accepted: .csv, .txt, .xlsx, .xls, .ods, up to 20 MB.</li><li>The <b>Import file</b> button, greyed out until a file is chosen; it reads "Importing…" while working.</li><li>When Extract B is selected, a blue notice explains that rows marked <b>Scheduled</b> are staged as the remaining schedule for comparison and never replace version 1, and rows marked <b>Actual</b> are kept as transaction evidence; a blank fee component does not block principal and interest.</li><li>A green banner "Contract transactions (Extract B) was detected from the file headers and selected automatically." when you chose another type but uploaded an Extract B file.</li><li>A status card while the job runs: <b>Import queued successfully</b>, then <b>Import processing</b>, then <b>Import completed</b> or <b>Import failed</b>, with the note "This page checks progress automatically; you do not need to refresh it.", the counts <b>Rows processed</b>, <b>Rows inserted</b> and <b>Contract exceptions</b>, and the links <b>Open Import History</b> and <b>Download exception report</b>.</li><li>Card <b>Import result</b> with tiles that depend on the type (below), a table of contract-level exceptions with columns <b>Contract</b>, <b>Status</b> (<code>held</code>, <code>rejected</code>, <code>incomplete</code> or <code>restated</code>) and <b>Reason</b>, a <b>Download CSV</b> link, and the line "Schedule coverage is now n/m contracts on the latest loan tape."</li></ul><h4>Field by field: required and optional columns</h4><ul><li><b>Contract master (Extract A)</b>: required <code>contract_id</code> only. Optional: run_id, customer_id, portfolio, sub_account_no, gl_account_code, currency, product_type, origination_date, first_repayment_date, maturity_date, closure_date, last_restructure_date, approved_amount, drawn_amount, contractual_rate, rate_basis, rate_type, reference_rate_at_origination, source_day_count_basis, source_compounding, disbursement_tranches, markup, repayment_frequency, payments_per_year, tenor_months, moratorium_months, arrangement_fee, legal_fees, opening_amortised_cost, opening_amortised_cost_date. A missing term does not reject the file; it appears later as a readiness blocker.</li><li><b>Repayment schedule</b>: required <code>contract_id</code>, <code>due_date</code>, <code>principal_due</code>, <code>interest_due</code>. Optional: fee_due.</li><li><b>Fees and transaction costs</b>: required <code>contract_id</code>, <code>fee_type</code>, <code>amount</code>. Optional: description, transaction_date, cashflow_direction, currency, source_system, source_reference, external_transaction_id, basis, gl_account_ref. Supply source_system and external_transaction_id so a re-import skips duplicates instead of loading them twice.</li><li><b>Contract transactions (Extract B)</b>: required <code>customer_id</code>, <code>contract_id</code>, <code>sub_account_no</code>, <code>transaction_date</code>, <code>transaction_type</code>, <code>principal_component</code>, <code>interest_component</code>, <code>total_amount</code>, <code>scheduled_actual_flag</code>, <code>gl_posting_ref</code>. Optional: run_id, fee_component, balance_after_transaction, row_note.</li><li><b>GL interest postings (Extract C)</b>: required <code>contract_id</code>, <code>period_year</code>, <code>period_month</code>, <code>interest_income_posted</code>. Optional: run_id, gl_account_code, period_type, reporting_period, transaction_count, posting_references, row_note, generated_on.</li></ul><p>Values are cleaned automatically: thousands separators and stray spaces are removed, bracketed amounts become negatives, Excel serial dates and dd-mm-yyyy or yyyy-mm-dd text are all read as dates, and the contract identifier is normalised so E-Banker and loan-tape padding agree.</p><h4>Result tiles by type</h4><ul><li>Extract A: <b>Contracts created</b>, <b>Terms updated</b>, <b>Unchanged</b>, <b>Origination fees → pending</b> (arrangement and legal fees routed to Fee Classification).</li><li>Repayment schedule: <b>Contracts loaded</b>, <b>Schedule rows</b>, <b>Held (not on tape)</b>, <b>Rejected</b>.</li><li>Fees: <b>Lines loaded as pending</b>, <b>Skipped (blank/zero)</b>, <b>Negative (netting) lines</b>, <b>Unknown fee types → other</b>, and a strip "Totals by fee type" to eyeball against the GL fee accounts.</li><li>Extract B: <b>Scheduled rows routed</b>, <b>Remaining rows staged</b>, <b>Rows not staged</b>, <b>Contracts staged</b>, then <b>New actual rows retained</b>, <b>Fee rows routed</b>, <b>Contracts held</b>, <b>Contracts rejected</b>. A red box "Scheduled rows were found, but none were staged." appears when every scheduled row failed.</li><li>Extract C: <b>Postings loaded</b>, <b>Annual totals excluded</b>, <b>GL restatements applied</b>, <b>Negative rows (check sign)</b>, <b>Total interest posted</b>, and a strip "Interest posted by period" to check against the GL before reconciling.</li></ul><h4>What happens next</h4><p>Each import appears in Import History named "EIR &lt;type&gt;: &lt;file name&gt;" and writes an audit entry. Extract A creates or updates the contract profiles seen on EIR Data; fees land in Fee Classification as PENDING with any rule suggestion attached; Extract B remaining rows feed the Schedule Review comparison; Extract C feeds the GL Reconciliation tab and page.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"The file is missing required columns: ... Check that the correct source type and file were selected."</b> The named fields could not be found under any known header. Rename the columns to the field names above, or ask the administrator to add a mapping template for that header.</li><li><b>Status "held" with reason "not on tape".</b> The contract is not on the latest loan book, so its schedule is parked until it is.</li><li><b>Status "incomplete".</b> The contract was created but lacks a term the solver needs; Schedule Review shows which.</li><li><b>Status "restated".</b> A GL figure replaced one for a period that may already have been reconciled; re-run the reconciliation.</li><li><b>"Could not refresh the status just now. Retrying automatically."</b> A momentary connection problem; the page keeps polling.</li><li><b>Import failed.</b> Open Import History and download the exception report for the row-level reasons.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open EIR Data and press EIR Data Intake.',
                'Choose the Import type that matches the file: Contract master (Extract A), Repayment schedule, Fees and transaction costs, Contract transactions (Extract B) or GL interest postings (Extract C).',
                'Press Choose File and select the CSV or Excel file (up to 20 MB).',
                'Press Import file. The platform reads the headers, checks the required columns and queues the job.',
                'Watch the status card move from Import queued successfully to Import processing to Import completed; the page refreshes itself.',
                'Read the Import result tiles and the contract-level exception table; press Download CSV or Download exception report for the detail.',
                'Load the files in month-end order: Extract A first, then fees and schedules, then Extract B, then Extract C.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-intake' => 'EIR Data Intake with the import type, file chooser and coverage pill'],
            'routes' => ['eir-intake.index', 'eir-intake.analyze', 'eir-intake.save-template', 'eir-intake.import', 'eir-intake.status'],
        ],

        'Reference rates and the spread over prime' => [
            'body' => '<p>Reference Rates shows the Reserve Bank prime lending rate (PLR) as a dated series: one row for every date the rate was recorded, oldest to newest, with the rate that applied from that day. MAIIC\'s Industrial and Agricultural loans are priced as the PLR plus a fixed number of percentage points, so when the Reserve Bank moves the PLR, those loans reprice (IFRS 9 B5.4.5). FinES loans are fixed at 10 percent for life and do not. The series is the one fact every floating-rate reset depends on, which is why it has its own screen and its own import checks. Open the sidebar, expand <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b> and choose <b>Reference Rates</b>.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li>Breadcrumb "EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition / Reference Rates", title <b>Reference Rates</b>, and header buttons <b>EIR Data</b> and <b>Import reference rates</b> (which opens EIR Data Intake with the type already set to <b>Reference rates (File C)</b>).</li><li>An <b>Index</b> selector, shown only when more than one series is loaded. Today there is one, PLR.</li><li>Six tiles: <b>PLR rate in force</b> with the date it took effect, <b>Rate changes</b> (including the opening rate), <b>Rows loaded</b> with how many of them repeat the previous rate, <b>First date</b>, <b>Last date</b>, and <b>Repaired rows</b>, the rows whose day and month had been swapped by Excel and were swapped back before loading.</li><li>The series table, newest first, with columns <b>Effective date</b>, <b>Rate</b>, <b>Change</b> (the move from the previous row in percentage points; "opening rate" on the first row and "no change" on a row that repeats the previous rate), <b>Source row</b>, <b>As delivered</b> (what the cell held in the file) and <b>How it was read</b>. A repaired row is marked in amber. Rows that are not rate changes are shown in grey.</li></ul><h4>Why the dates must be written year first</h4><p>The rate table came out of E-Banker with dates such as 03/04/2020. That is 3 April in Malawi and 4 March in a spreadsheet set to American dates, and Excel silently chose the second reading for 20 of the 48 rows. A reference rate on the wrong date puts the wrong rate in force for every loan for weeks. The importer therefore accepts only dates written year first (2020-04-03) and refuses the whole file, naming the column and the first bad row, if any date is written another way. It never guesses which of day and month is which. Dates must also rise strictly down the file; the first row out of order is named and the file is refused. A row that repeats the previous rate is loaded and counted, but it is not a rate change. A date already stored with a different rate is never overwritten: the row is listed as skipped so a person can decide.</p><h4>How the spread over prime is worked out</h4><p>The contractual rate on a PLR-linked loan is the PLR plus the spread added to the prime rate (margin), a fixed number of percentage points agreed in the offer letter. E-Banker does not export that spread as a field, so the system derives it from the evidence it already has. For every month a loan appears in the loan book, it takes the rate the loan book shows and subtracts the PLR in force at that month end. If the result is the same in every month (to within 0.15 percentage points) the loan has a constant spread, and the median of those monthly values is stored as the derived spread. Over December 2025 to August 2026 every PLR-linked loan passed this test: the rate moved by exactly the PLR change in each of eight months, landing on clean spreads such as 4.90, 5.00, 6.00 and 7.70 points.</p><h4>How the spread is checked</h4><p>A loan whose monthly spread wanders by more than 0.15 percentage points is not given a spread. It is flagged for review, because a wrong spread would misprice every reset from then on. A fixed-rate loan naturally shows a moving spread as the PLR moves, which is expected and is said so in the note. Where MAIIC has supplied its own figure for the spread, it is compared with the derived one and the result is recorded, but the supplied figure is only a cross-check: the engine does not depend on a field the core system does not hold. Both figures sit side by side on the contract record so an auditor can see them together.</p><h4>To load a new rate file</h4><ol><li>Get the rate table from E-Banker (the interest rates by effective date) and save it as a CSV with the dates written year first, for example 2026-09-03.</li><li>Press <b>Import reference rates</b>. The intake screen opens with the type set to <b>Reference rates (File C)</b>.</li><li>Choose the file. The columns Effective date and Rate map by name; Index, Source row, As delivered and Interpretation are optional.</li><li>Press <b>Import file</b>. The result shows rates loaded, rows already stored, rate changes in the file, rows repeating the previous rate, and the rate now in force.</li><li>If the file is refused, read the message: it names the column and the row. Fix the file and load it again; nothing from a refused file is stored.</li><li>Open <b>Reference Rates</b> and check the rate in force and the last date against the source.</li></ol><h4>From the command line</h4><p><code>php artisan eir:import-reference-rates path/to/file.csv --user=ID</code> loads a file with the same checks; <code>--index=PLR</code> names the series when the file carries no index column. <code>php artisan eir:derive-spreads</code> derives the spread for every contract from the loan books and lists the ones that drift; <code>--period=YYYY-MM</code> reads loan-book months up to and including that month.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"effective_date on row N is \'03/04/2020\', which is not a date written year first."</b> Re-save the file with ISO dates. Do not let Excel reformat the column; keep it as text.</li><li><b>"effective_date on row N is earlier than row M."</b> The file is out of order, often because a transposed date jumped months. Sort by date and check that row.</li><li><b>A rate is listed as skipped with "stored rate differs from the file\'s".</b> The stored series already has that date at another rate. Confirm which is right with Credit before anything is changed.</li><li><b>Every loan is held for a drifting spread.</b> The series is probably missing a rate change, so the month-end PLR is wrong for a stretch of months. Check the last date on this screen against the Reserve Bank\'s announcements.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open Reference Rates from the EIR & Revenue Recognition group and read the rate in force, the last date and the number of rate changes.',
                'To load a new file, press Import reference rates; the intake opens on the Reference rates (File C) type.',
                'Make sure every date in the file is written year first (yyyy-mm-dd) and the rows rise by date.',
                'Choose the file and press Import file. Read the result tiles and any refusal message, which names the column and the row.',
                'Back on Reference Rates, confirm the new rows appear with the right change from the previous rate.',
                'Run php artisan eir:derive-spreads after the month\'s loan book is in, and review any contract listed as drifting.',
            ],
            'routes' => ['eir-reference-rates.index'],
        ],

        'Fee classification' => [
            'body' => '<p>Fee Classification is the work queue where every imported fee or cost line is decided integral or non-integral, with a written accounting reason, and then reviewed by a second person. Until every line on a loan is reviewed (or rejected) the loan cannot be solved: readiness reports FEE_CLASSIFICATION_PENDING. Finance officers classify; reviewers approve. Open the sidebar, expand <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b> and choose <b>Fee Classification</b>.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li>Breadcrumb "EIR Data / Fee Classification", title <b>EIR Fee &amp; Cost Classification</b>, strapline "Classify imported lines, then independently review them before EIR calculation".</li><li>Four tiles, <b>PENDING lines</b>, <b>CLASSIFIED lines</b>, <b>REVIEWED lines</b> and <b>REJECTED lines</b>, each with the number of lines and their total amount.</li><li>Filter bar: a text box "Contract/account", a status dropdown (<code>All statuses</code> plus the four statuses), a type dropdown (<code>All types</code>, arrangement, legal, appraisal, default, levy, other) and <b>Apply filters</b>.</li><li>Card <b>Accounting rulebook</b> reading "N approved rule(s), M awaiting approval. X of Y pending line(s) carry a matched rule." (or "No pending lines."), an amber line "N pending line(s) have no matched rule. Re-apply after approving rules, or classify them manually." when relevant, and the button <b>Re-apply rules to pending lines</b>.</li><li>A selection bar, shown once you tick a line: "N selected line(s)", a note "N without a matched rule", and the buttons <b>Classify using each line\'s rule</b> (greyed out when none of the selection has a rule), <b>Classify manually</b> and <b>Review/approve</b>.</li><li>Table columns: a select-all checkbox, <b>Contract / reference</b>, <b>Fee or cost</b> (type, description and direction), <b>Amount</b> (with currency), <b>Rule suggestion</b> (rule name and an <b>Integral</b> or <b>Non-integral</b> badge, or "No matching rule"), <b>Decision</b> (the recorded treatment badge and the reason) and <b>Status</b>. Reviewed lines cannot be ticked. Empty state: "No fee or cost lines match these filters."</li><li>Ordering: PENDING first, then CLASSIFIED, then the rest, newest first.</li></ul><h4>Status values</h4><ul><li><b>PENDING</b>: imported, no decision yet.</li><li><b>CLASSIFIED</b>: a maker has recorded a treatment and reason; awaiting review.</li><li><b>REVIEWED</b>: a checker has approved the decision. Only reviewed integral lines enter the EIR.</li><li><b>REJECTED</b>: set aside. The readiness check treats it as resolved. No button on this screen produces it today.</li></ul><h4>The two classify modes</h4><p><b>Classify manually</b> applies one treatment to every selected line: use it when you have judged the selection as a set. Fields: <b>EIR treatment</b> (<code>Choose treatment</code>, <code>Integral - include in EIR</code>, <code>Non-integral - exclude</code>), <b>Accounting rule</b> (<code>No linked rule</code> or an approved rule to cite) and <b>Accounting reason</b>. <b>Classify by rule</b> gives each line the treatment of its own matched rule, so a mixed selection of arrangement fees and default charges resolves correctly in one action; the modal explains "Each line takes the treatment from its own matched rule" and how many will be classified and how many "have no matched rule and will be skipped, not guessed." Only the <b>Accounting reason</b> is asked for, and the rule name is appended to it as "[applied by rule: name]". Both modals end with <b>Cancel</b> and <b>Save classification</b>.</p><h4>To review classifications</h4><ol><li>Filter by status CLASSIFIED and tick the lines you have checked.</li><li>Press <b>Review/approve</b>. The modal <b>Approve classifications</b> reads "Approve the classifications for N selected line(s)? You cannot review decisions that you classified yourself."</li><li>Press <b>Approve classifications</b>. The message reads "N classification(s) approved." and the lines become REVIEWED.</li></ol><h4>To re-apply the rulebook</h4><ol><li>Approve or activate the rules on Accounting Rules.</li><li>Press <b>Re-apply rules to pending lines</b>. The message reads "Re-applied the rulebook to N pending line(s): a matched, b still unmatched, c suggestion(s) updated." and, where relevant, "d already-classified line(s) were left untouched." or "No pending fee lines to re-match."</li></ol><h4>Administrator override and the event trail</h4><p>A user with the admin role may review lines they classified; the audit entry "EIR Fee Classifications Reviewed" then carries the flag maker_checker_override. Every classify and review writes an event on the fee line (action, treatment, reason, linked rule, who and when) and an audit log entry ("EIR Fees Classified", "EIR Fee Classifications Reviewed", "EIR Fee Rules Re-applied"). Classifying a line again before review replaces the decision and clears any earlier reviewer.</p><h4>What happens next</h4><p>Reviewed integral lines adjust the loan\'s initial net investment: amounts received by MAIIC reduce it, amounts paid increase it. A reviewed integral line must have a direction (RECEIVED or PAID), otherwise readiness reports FEE_DIRECTION_MISSING. Once every line on a loan is REVIEWED or REJECTED the fee blocker clears and the loan can be solved.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"A classifier cannot review their own decision."</b> Ask a colleague to review, or an administrator to override.</li><li><b>"No line could be classified: none of the selected lines has a matching rule. Re-apply the rulebook, or classify them manually."</b> Rule mode found no rule on any selected line.</li><li><b>"Enter the accounting reason."</b> and <b>"Choose a treatment."</b> The modal will not save without them.</li><li><b>"N line(s) were skipped for having no matching rule: ..."</b> The named lines stay PENDING; classify them manually.</li><li><b>The rulebook card says "0 approved rule(s), N awaiting approval".</b> Rules exist but none is approved, so no line can carry a suggestion.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open the sidebar, expand EIR & Revenue Recognition and choose Fee Classification.',
                'Set the filters (status PENDING, a fee type or a contract) and press Apply filters.',
                'Tick the lines to decide, or use the checkbox in the table header to select every unreviewed line on the page.',
                'Press Classify using each line\'s rule when the Rule suggestion column is filled, or Classify manually to choose the treatment yourself.',
                'In the modal choose the EIR treatment (manual mode), optionally cite an Accounting rule, and type the Accounting reason.',
                'Press Save classification. The lines become CLASSIFIED and show your reason in the Decision column.',
                'A second person filters by CLASSIFIED, ticks the lines, presses Review/approve and then Approve classifications.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-fees' => 'The fee classification work queue with status tiles, filters and the rulebook card'],
            'routes' => ['eir-fee-classification.index', 'eir-fee-classification.rematch', 'eir-fee-classification.classify', 'eir-fee-classification.review'],
        ],

        'EIR calculations, approval and reopening' => [
            'body' => '<p>EIR Calculations solves each loan\'s original effective interest rate, records the evidence, and lets a second person approve and lock it. The solver finds the single rate at which the version-1 cash flows, discounted back to origination, equal the initial net investment (the drawn amount less integral fees received plus integral costs paid). Finance officers request calculations; reviewers approve and lock; administrators reopen. Open the sidebar, expand <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b> and choose <b>EIR Calculations</b>.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li>Breadcrumb "EIR Data / Calculations", title <b>EIR Calculations</b>, strapline "Calculate, independently approve, and lock each contract\'s original effective interest rate".</li><li>Five tiles: <b>PENDING contracts</b>, <b>REOPENED contracts</b>, <b>BLOCKED contracts</b>, <b>CALCULATED contracts</b>, <b>LOCKED contracts</b>.</li><li>Card <b>Bulk approval</b> (shown only when at least one calculated, unlocked contract exists) reading "N calculated contract(s) are eligible across all pages." followed by either "Administrator override is active." or "X calculated by you and Y without maker evidence will be skipped.", and the button <b>Approve &amp; lock all eligible (N)</b>.</li><li>Filter bar: text box "Contract, account or reference", status dropdown (<code>All statuses</code>, PENDING, REOPENED, BLOCKED, CALCULATED, LOCKED), <b>Apply filters</b>, <b>Clear filters</b>, the line "Showing a, b of n matching records." and a <b>Filters active</b> badge.</li><li>Selection bar "N contract(s) selected" with <b>Calculate selected</b>.</li><li>Table columns: a checkbox (absent on locked rows), <b>Contract</b> (currency beneath), <b>Status</b> (badge, with the blocking message in red beneath when BLOCKED), <b>Periodic EIR</b>, <b>Effective annual EIR</b>, <b>Evidence</b> (Calculated date and time, solver method and iteration count, Locked date and time) and <b>Action</b>.</li><li>Action buttons: <b>Approve &amp; lock</b> (CALCULATED rows), <b>Calculate</b> (PENDING), <b>Retry calculation</b> (BLOCKED), <b>Recalculate</b> (CALCULATED or REOPENED), <b>Reopen locked EIR</b> (locked rows, administrators only) or the text "Locked, administrator reopening required".</li><li>Rows are ordered BLOCKED, REOPENED, CALCULATED, PENDING, LOCKED, most recently changed first. Empty state: "No contracts match these filters."</li><li>Reopen modal <b>Reopen locked original EIR</b>: the contract, an amber warning "The locked calculation will be archived. Revenue-recognition rows will be superseded and discounted ECL will be marked stale. The EIR must then be recalculated and independently approved.", the field <b>Reason for reopening</b> (at least 10 characters, up to 500, "recorded in audit history"), and the buttons <b>Cancel</b> and <b>Reopen and invalidate results</b>.</li></ul><h4>Status values</h4><ul><li><b>PENDING</b>: never calculated.</li><li><b>CALCULATED</b>: solved, awaiting approval. Can be recalculated; the earlier result is archived with the note "Recalculated before final approval."</li><li><b>BLOCKED</b>: the readiness check or the solver failed; the reason is shown under the badge.</li><li><b>LOCKED</b>: approved by a second person; permanent unless reopened.</li><li><b>REOPENED</b>: an administrator reopened a lock; the rate has been cleared and must be solved and approved again.</li></ul><h4>Readiness: READY or BLOCKED</h4><p>Before solving, the platform assesses the contract. It is READY when no issue is found, otherwise BLOCKED with one or more codes: CONTRACT_PROFILE_MISSING ("EIR contract profile is missing."), EQUITY_EXCLUDED, EIR_LOCKED, ORIGINATION_DATE_MISSING, DRAWN_AMOUNT_MISSING, FREQUENCY_INVALID, FREQUENCY_ASSUMED ("Payment frequency was assumed, not stated by a source. Confirm it against the contract master or offer letter before solving."), SCHEDULE_NOT_APPROVED, ORIGINAL_SCHEDULE_MISSING, SCHEDULE_INVALID, SCHEDULE_DATE_INVALID, SCHEDULE_DATE_DUPLICATE, PRINCIPAL_NOT_RECONCILED (scheduled principal differs from drawn by more than 1%), FEE_CLASSIFICATION_PENDING, FEE_DIRECTION_MISSING and INITIAL_NET_INVALID. Coverage &amp; Blockers lists the same codes in plain English.</p><h4>To approve and lock one contract</h4><ol><li>Check the <b>Effective annual EIR</b> against expectation and the <b>Evidence</b> column for the maker\'s name is not yours.</li><li>Press <b>Approve &amp; lock</b> and confirm "Approve and permanently lock the original EIR for &lt;contract&gt;?".</li><li>The message reads "Original EIR for &lt;contract&gt; approved and locked." and the status becomes LOCKED.</li></ol><h4>To approve and lock in bulk</h4><ol><li>Read the Bulk approval card: contracts you calculated yourself, and contracts with no recorded maker, are skipped unless you are an administrator.</li><li>Press <b>Approve &amp; lock all eligible (N)</b> and confirm "Approve and permanently lock all N eligible calculated EIRs across every page? This cannot be undone."</li><li>The message reads "N EIR(s) approved and permanently locked." with "M contract(s) were skipped by maker-checker or approval validation." when some were.</li></ol><h4>What a lock means, and what reopening invalidates</h4><p>A locked EIR is the loan\'s original rate for its whole life. It drives the monthly revenue run and the discount rate used for that loan\'s ECL. Reopening archives the locked calculation to history with your reason, moves every monthly revenue row for the loan to history, clears the loan\'s discounted ECL fields on the loan book and marks them STALE_EIR_REOPENED, and sets the status to REOPENED with the rate blank. The audit log entry "Locked Original EIR Reopened" records the old rate, the reason and that downstream results were invalidated.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"The calculator cannot approve and lock their own EIR."</b> A different person must approve, or an administrator overrides.</li><li><b>"The calculation has no identifiable maker and cannot be approved."</b> The rate was solved without a recorded user; recalculate it so a maker is recorded.</li><li><b>"Only a successfully calculated EIR can be approved and locked."</b> The row is not CALCULATED.</li><li><b>"There are no calculated, unlocked EIRs to approve."</b> and <b>"No EIRs were locked. N contract(s) failed maker-checker or approval validation."</b> Nothing was eligible for bulk approval.</li><li><b>"A locked original EIR cannot be recalculated."</b> Reopen it first.</li><li><b>"Only a locked original EIR can be reopened."</b> and <b>"A specific reopening reason of at least 10 characters is required."</b></li><li><b>"Only an administrator can reopen a locked original EIR."</b> Ask an administrator.</li><li><b>Red text under a BLOCKED badge.</b> Fix the named issue through intake, Schedule Review or Fee Classification, then press Retry calculation.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open the sidebar, expand EIR & Revenue Recognition and choose EIR Calculations.',
                'Filter by status PENDING (or REOPENED) and press Apply filters.',
                'Tick the contracts to solve, or use the header checkbox for every unlocked row on the page.',
                'Press Calculate selected. The message reads "N contract(s) queued for EIR calculation." and the job runs in the background.',
                'Refresh the page after a moment: each row shows CALCULATED with its Periodic EIR, Effective annual EIR and solver evidence, or BLOCKED with the reason in red.',
                'A second person presses Approve & lock on each row (or Approve & lock all eligible) and confirms the prompt.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-calculations' => 'EIR Calculations with the five status tiles, filters and the contract table'],
            'routes' => ['eir-calculations.index', 'eir-calculations.calculate', 'eir-calculations.approve-all', 'eir-calculations.approve', 'eir-calculations.reopen'],
        ],

        'GL reconciliation' => [
            'body' => '<p>GL Reconciliation explains, for one month, why the interest income calculated at the EIR differs from the interest the general ledger posted. A bare variance invites the assumption that the engine is wrong; this page splits the difference into three named effects that add up exactly, so you can see which part is a measurement difference and which part needs investigating. Finance and the reviewer use it after the monthly revenue run and the Extract C import. Open the sidebar, expand <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b> and choose <b>GL Reconciliation</b>.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li>Breadcrumb "EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition / GL Reconciliation", title <b>EIR to GL Reconciliation</b>, strapline "Why calculated interest income differs from what the ledger posted".</li><li>Header buttons <b>GL Postings</b> (the GL Reconciliation tab of EIR Data) and <b>EIR Calculations</b>.</li><li>Filter bar: <b>Reporting period</b> (months that have GL postings, newest first), <b>Portfolio</b> (<code>All portfolios</code> or one) and <b>Apply</b>.</li><li>Before any Extract C is loaded: "No GL interest postings have been loaded yet. Use EIR Data Intake to load an interest posting extract."</li><li>Four cards: <b>GL interest posted</b>, <b>EIR interest calculated</b>, <b>Net variance</b> and <b>Rows within tolerance</b> ("a of b", where b excludes rows with no calculation).</li><li>Card <b>Variance bridge</b> for the period, with lines: <b>GL interest posted, all rows</b>; <b>Postings with no calculated counterpart</b> (with "n row(s)"); <b>GL interest on matched facilities</b>; <b>Base effect</b>; <b>Rate effect</b>; <b>Impairment effect</b>; <b>Unexplained</b>; <b>EIR interest calculated</b>. Under it either "The three effects account for the variance in full, no unexplained residual." or "&lt;amount&gt; is not explained by any of the three effects, for at least one facility the ledger did not post at the contractual rate at all. Review the rows below."</li><li>An amber notice <b>The rate effect is effectively nil</b> when no integral fees have been classified yet: the whole variance is then a balance difference.</li><li>Card <b>Facilities</b>, "Sorted by absolute variance. Tolerance is ±1% of the posted amount.", with columns <b>Contract</b> (portfolio and GL account beneath), <b>Basis</b> (Opening balance, Drawn amount, GROSS or NET), <b>GL posted</b>, <b>EIR accrued</b>, <b>Variance</b> (with the percentage beneath), <b>GL implied base</b>, <b>Base effect</b>, <b>Rate effect</b>, <b>Impairment</b> and <b>Status</b>. Negative amounts are shown in brackets. Empty state: "No GL postings for this period and portfolio."</li></ul><h4>Status values</h4><ul><li><b>Within tolerance</b>: the variance is no more than 1% of the posted amount (with a floor of MWK 1, so tiny postings do not register a false variance).</li><li><b>Variance</b>: outside that band.</li><li><b>Not calculated</b>: the loan has an EIR profile but no revenue row for this month (the EIR is not locked, or the run has not been done).</li><li><b>No EIR contract</b>: the posting names a loan with no Extract A profile at all.</li></ul><p>Rows with no calculated counterpart are shown but kept out of the bridge, because a missing row is a coverage gap, not a measurement difference.</p><h4>The three effects in plain words</h4><ul><li><b>Base effect.</b> The two sides accrued interest on different balances. The platform works out the balance the ledger must have used (<b>GL implied base</b> = posted interest divided by the contractual monthly rate) and compares it with the amortised opening balance the engine used. A ledger that posts flat interest on the original principal, and one that accrues on the declining balance, both decompose cleanly.</li><li><b>Rate effect.</b> The yield uplift from fees classified as integral: the difference between the effective monthly rate and the contractual monthly rate, applied to the opening balance. This is the amount the EIR engine exists to recognise, and it is zero until integral fees are reviewed.</li><li><b>Impairment effect.</b> A Stage 3 loan accrues interest on the amortised cost net of its loss allowance rather than on the gross balance (the NET basis). The shortfall against a gross accrual is a correct measurement difference under IFRS 9, not an error. It is zero for every performing loan.</li><li><b>Unexplained.</b> Whatever is left, most often because the ledger did not post at the contractual rate at all for that loan. It is reported, never absorbed.</li></ul><h4>To work a reconciliation</h4><ol><li>Load Extract C for the month through EIR Data Intake and ask the administrator to run the monthly revenue for the same month.</li><li>Choose the <b>Reporting period</b> and, if useful, a <b>Portfolio</b>, and press <b>Apply</b>.</li><li>Read the bridge. If the footer reports no unexplained residual, the variance is fully described by the three effects.</li><li>Work down the Facilities table from the largest variance. A large base effect points at the ledger\'s accrual basis; a large impairment figure is a Stage 3 loan; an unexplained figure needs the posting checked.</li><li>Record the conclusions in the Period Workspace for the close.</li></ol><h4>What happens next</h4><p>Nothing on this page writes to the database; it is a reading of the revenue rows and the GL postings. Re-importing Extract C (restatements) or re-running revenue for the month changes the figures immediately.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Every row says Not calculated.</b> The revenue run has not been done for this month, or the loans are not locked. See Running the monthly revenue.</li><li><b>Rows say No EIR contract.</b> Extract A has not been loaded for those loans, or the identifiers differ; check the contract identifier in both files.</li><li><b>The rate effect is nil.</b> Fee lines are not yet reviewed as integral, so the solved EIR equals the contractual rate. Finish Fee Classification, reopen and re-solve if the rate was already locked.</li><li><b>The GL implied base looks like the original principal on every row.</b> The ledger is posting flat interest and not amortising; that is a finding for the accounting team, not a platform error.</li></ul>',
            'images' => ['eir-reconciliation' => 'EIR to GL Reconciliation with the period and portfolio filters'],
            'routes' => ['eir-reconciliation.index'],
        ],

        'Coverage and blockers' => [
            'body' => '<p>Coverage &amp; Blockers answers a portfolio-level question: how much of the loan book carries a locked EIR, and what is stopping the rest. It counts contracts and weighs them by carrying amount, because a blocker affecting thousands of dormant facilities and one affecting a handful of large facilities look identical in a count. Management and the project team use it to prioritise data requests to the bank. Open the sidebar, expand <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b> and choose <b>Coverage &amp; Blockers</b>.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li>Breadcrumb "EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition / Coverage &amp; Blockers", title <b>EIR Coverage &amp; Blockers</b>, strapline "How much of the book carries a locked EIR, and what is holding back the rest".</li><li>Header buttons <b>EIR Calculations</b> and <b>GL Reconciliation</b>.</li><li>Filter bar: <b>Reporting period</b> (months on the loan book, newest first), <b>Portfolio</b> (<code>All portfolios</code> or one) and <b>Apply</b>.</li><li>Two headline cards: <b>Coverage by contract count</b> with a percentage, a progress bar and "n of m in-scope contracts carry a locked EIR"; and <b>Coverage by exposure</b> with a percentage, a bar and "x of y carrying amount". The percentage is green at 90% or more, amber at 50% or more, otherwise red.</li><li>A green notice <b>Covered facilities are disproportionately large</b> when exposure coverage runs more than one and a half times ahead of contract coverage.</li><li>Five state tiles, each with a count and an exposure: <b>Locked</b>, <b>Awaiting approval</b> (CALCULATED), <b>Ready to solve</b> (READY), <b>Blocked</b> and <b>Out of scope</b> (equity-excluded instruments).</li><li>Card <b>Blockers, ranked by exposure</b> with columns <b>Blocker</b> (plain-English label with the code beneath), <b>Contracts</b>, <b>Exposure</b>, <b>% of book</b>, <b>Sole blocker</b> and a <b>Show</b> link (which becomes <b>Clear</b>) that filters the facilities table to that blocker. Empty state: "No blockers, every in-scope contract is solvable."</li><li>Card <b>By portfolio</b> with columns <b>Portfolio</b>, <b>Contracts</b>, <b>Covered</b>, <b>Coverage</b> and <b>Exposure</b>, largest exposure first; contracts without a portfolio appear as "Unassigned".</li><li>Card <b>Largest affected facilities</b>, "Showing the 50 largest by exposure of N.", with a <b>Clear blocker filter</b> button when a blocker is selected, and columns <b>Contract</b> (with "Not on the current tape" when the loan has a profile but is missing from the month\'s loan book), <b>Portfolio</b>, <b>Exposure</b>, <b>State</b> and <b>Blockers</b> (codes). Empty state: "No contracts match."</li></ul><h4>What "sole blocker" means</h4><p>The Sole blocker column counts the contracts for which this is the only thing holding them back. Clearing that one blocker makes exactly those contracts solvable, which is not true of contracts with several blockers. When choosing what to ask the bank for first, sort by exposure and then look at sole blocker: the highest exposure with the highest sole-blocker count is the best return on effort.</p><h4>Blocker codes in plain English</h4><ul><li><b>CONTRACT_PROFILE_MISSING</b>: No EIR contract profile exists (Extract A has not been loaded for this loan).</li><li><b>ORIGINATION_DATE_MISSING</b>: Origination/value date is missing.</li><li><b>DRAWN_AMOUNT_MISSING</b>: Drawn amount is missing or zero.</li><li><b>FREQUENCY_INVALID</b>: Payment frequency is not a supported value (annual, semi-annual, quarterly, bi-monthly or monthly).</li><li><b>FREQUENCY_ASSUMED</b>: Payment frequency was assumed, not stated by a source.</li><li><b>SCHEDULE_NOT_APPROVED</b>: Original schedule is a draft awaiting review and approval.</li><li><b>ORIGINAL_SCHEDULE_MISSING</b>: Original contractual schedule (version 1) is missing.</li><li><b>SCHEDULE_INVALID</b>: Schedule has a missing date or negative cash flow.</li><li><b>SCHEDULE_DATE_INVALID</b>: A scheduled cash flow falls on or before origination.</li><li><b>SCHEDULE_DATE_DUPLICATE</b>: Schedule contains duplicate due dates.</li><li><b>PRINCIPAL_NOT_RECONCILED</b>: Scheduled principal does not reconcile to drawn within 1%.</li><li><b>FEE_CLASSIFICATION_PENDING</b>: Fee/cost lines are not independently reviewed.</li><li><b>FEE_DIRECTION_MISSING</b>: A reviewed integral fee has no cash-flow direction.</li><li><b>INITIAL_NET_INVALID</b>: Fee-adjusted initial net investment is not positive.</li></ul><p>The checks are the same ones EIR Calculations applies loan by loan, with one deliberate difference: a locked EIR is reported here as the goal state, not as a blocker.</p><h4>To find what to fix first</h4><ol><li>Choose the latest <b>Reporting period</b> and press <b>Apply</b>.</li><li>Read the two headline percentages. If exposure coverage is well ahead of contract coverage, the important loans are already done.</li><li>In the blockers table, press <b>Show</b> on the top row. The facilities table now lists the largest loans held by that blocker.</li><li>Raise the data request for those loans, then press <b>Clear</b> and move to the next blocker.</li><li>Use <b>By portfolio</b> to report progress to each portfolio owner.</li></ol><h4>What happens next</h4><p>The page reads live data, so it moves as soon as an import, an approval or a lock lands. Nothing on it writes to the database.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Coverage is 0% and every loan shows CONTRACT_PROFILE_MISSING.</b> Extract A has not been loaded yet, or its contract identifiers do not match the loan book\'s.</li><li><b>A loan shows "Not on the current tape".</b> It has an EIR profile but is absent from the selected month\'s loan book; its exposure is shown as zero. Check the loan book import for that month.</li><li><b>The period dropdown is empty.</b> No loan book has been imported; coverage is measured against the loan tape.</li></ul>',
            'images' => ['eir-coverage' => 'Coverage & Blockers with coverage by count and exposure, state tiles and the blocker ranking'],
            'routes' => ['eir-coverage.index'],
        ],

        'Running the monthly revenue' => [
            'body' => '<p>The monthly revenue run rolls every locked loan forward one month at its EIR: opening balance, interest accrued, cash received, closing balance. Its output is the calculated interest that the GL Reconciliation tab and page compare against Extract C. There is no button for it yet: it is run from the command line by a developer or the system administrator, after the month\'s loan book, Extract B and Extract C are in. Amortised cost, for this purpose, is the balance the loan is carried at: what was advanced, plus interest accrued at the EIR, less cash collected.</p><h4>The command</h4><p><code>php artisan eir:run-revenue 2025-11</code> runs November 2025 for every locked contract. Options: <code>--contract=ID</code> (repeatable) limits the run to named loans; <code>--queue</code> puts the job on the queue instead of running it in the terminal; <code>--recalculate</code> replaces rows that already exist for the period; <code>--reason="..."</code> is required with --recalculate.</p><h4>What the run needs</h4><ul><li>A locked EIR: otherwise the loan is BLOCKED with "The original EIR must be approved and locked before revenue can be run."</li><li>A loan-book row for the loan in that month: otherwise "No loan-book snapshot exists for &lt;period&gt;."</li><li>A valid IFRS 9 stage (1, 2 or 3) on that row: otherwise "The IFRS 9 stage for &lt;period&gt; is missing or invalid."</li></ul><h4>What it produces</h4><p>One amortisation row per loan per month holding: <b>opening gross</b> (the previous month\'s closing, or for the first month the initial net investment from the locked snapshot, discounted forward to the month end where the loan started earlier); <b>interest accrued</b> at the monthly EIR (the annual effective rate converted to a monthly rate); <b>interest basis</b> GROSS for Stage 1 and 2 or NET for Stage 3, where interest is accrued on the balance net of the ECL allowance; <b>unwind amount</b> (the discount unwind on the allowance for Stage 3 loans); <b>cash received</b> and its <b>cash source</b>; <b>closing gross</b>; and the <b>ECL allowance</b> used. Cash received comes from Extract B actual rows of type Interest, Principal+Interest or Fee within the month (source IMPORTED); Disbursement rows are cash advanced and never netted off; any other type is counted as unclassified cash and reported rather than absorbed. Where the Extract B actuals do not cover the month at all, the version-1 schedule\'s due amounts are used instead and the row is labelled DERIVED, so a promise can be told from a receipt.</p><h4>What the terminal shows</h4><p>"EIR revenue run completed for &lt;period&gt;." followed by a table with <b>Requested</b>, <b>Created</b>, <b>Recalculated</b>, <b>Unchanged</b>, <b>Blocked</b>, <b>Rows superseded</b>, <b>Cash from schedule</b> and <b>Unclassified cash</b>, then, if any, "N contract(s) produced no amortisation row:" with a Contract and Reason table. An audit entry "EIR Revenue Run Completed" (or "EIR Revenue Recalculated") stores the same summary and the blocked list.</p><h4>The reason rule for recalculation</h4><p>A row that already exists for the month is left alone and reported as UNCHANGED. To replace it you must pass both <code>--recalculate</code> and <code>--reason</code>; without the reason the command refuses with "--recalculate requires --reason: a restated figure must carry its justification." The replaced rows are copied to history with the reason, who ran it and when, so a restated figure can always be traced.</p><h4>Recalculation supersedes later periods</h4><p>Each month\'s opening balance is the previous month\'s closing, so restating one month invalidates every month after it for that loan. The command warns: "Recalculating &lt;period&gt; also voids every later period for each affected contract, because each opening balance is the prior closing. Re-run those periods in order afterwards." All rows from the restated month onwards are archived and removed; the <b>Rows superseded</b> count includes them. Run the later months again, in order, to rebuild the chain. Reopening a locked EIR from EIR Calculations has the same effect on every month of that loan.</p><h4>To run a month end</h4><ol><li>Confirm the loan book for the month is imported and staged, and Extract B and Extract C are loaded through EIR Data Intake.</li><li>Confirm the loans you expect are LOCKED on EIR Calculations.</li><li>Run <code>php artisan eir:run-revenue YYYY-MM</code> from the application folder.</li><li>Read the summary table; investigate every blocked contract and any unclassified cash.</li><li>Open GL Reconciliation, choose the period and review the bridge.</li></ol><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>"Period must use YYYY-MM format."</b> Type the period as, for example, 2025-11.</li><li><b>"The locked EIR snapshot has no positive initial net investment."</b> The locked calculation is unusable; reopen, correct the fees and re-solve.</li><li><b>"No remaining contractual cash flows exist after &lt;period&gt;."</b> The loan\'s schedule ends before the requested month; it should have matured.</li><li><b>"A recalculation must state a reason."</b> The job was dispatched with --recalculate but an empty reason.</li><li><b>Unchanged is high.</b> The month was already run; use --recalculate with a reason only if the inputs have genuinely changed.</li><li><b>Cash from schedule is high.</b> Extract B actuals do not cover the month for those loans; load the transactions extract before relying on the figures.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Check that the month\'s loan book, Extract B and Extract C are imported and that the loans are LOCKED.',
                'Open a terminal in the application folder.',
                'Run: php artisan eir:run-revenue YYYY-MM (add --queue to run in the background).',
                'Read the summary table: Requested, Created, Recalculated, Unchanged, Blocked, Rows superseded, Cash from schedule and Unclassified cash.',
                'Resolve each blocked contract using the reason shown, then run the command again for those contracts with --contract=ID.',
                'To restate a month, run it again with --recalculate --reason="..." and then re-run every later month in order.',
                'Open GL Reconciliation for the period to review the result.',
            ],
            'images' => ['eir-reconciliation' => 'The GL reconciliation that reads the monthly revenue rows'],
        ],

        'Governance Centre' => [
            'body' => '<p>The Governance Centre is where the conventions the EIR engine calculates with are kept. Open the sidebar, expand <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b> and choose <b>Governance Centre</b>. It needs the EIR govern permission.</p><h4>What a setting is</h4><p>A setting is one calculation convention with a fixed list of options: for example the day count (actual/365 or 30/360), what happens when the prime lending rate changes inside a month, or the band inside which the engine\'s interest and the ledger\'s posting count as agreeing. The page shows each setting with the option in force today, the date it took effect, who proposed it and who approved it, and every value it has ever had. The first value of every setting is Dupleix\'s recommendation, seeded with the system; MAIIC can change any of them. Nothing is written in the program: if a setting has no approved value, the calculation that needs it stops and names the setting that is missing.</p><h4>Why a change needs an approver</h4><p>A change is made in two steps by two people. One person proposes it: the new option, the date it takes effect and the reason, in plain words. A second person approves it. The person who proposed a change cannot approve it; an administrator can, as an override, and the override is recorded. The audit log keeps the setting, the old value, the new value, who proposed, who approved, when, why and the first month it applies to, so an auditor can see every convention and every change without asking anyone.</p><h4>Why a locked period keeps its settings</h4><p>A change applies from its effective date forward only, and the effective date must be later than the last approved change to the same setting. A month that was run under the old value keeps it: the engine looks up the value that was in force on the last day of the month it is calculating, not the value in force today. That is what makes a locked period repeatable, and it is why the old value stays on the page as history instead of being overwritten.</p>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open Governance Centre from the EIR & Revenue Recognition group in the sidebar.',
                'Read the value in force for the setting, its effective date and its approver. A red badge means no value is approved and the calculation that needs it will stop.',
                'To change a setting press Propose change, choose the new option, set the effective date, write the reason and press Propose change.',
                'Ask a second person with the govern permission to open the page and press Approve beside the proposal.',
                'Press History to see every value the setting has had and the audit copies of the values that were superseded.',
            ],
            'routes' => ['eir-governance.index'],
        ],

        'How the interest reconciliation works' => [
            'body' => '<p>Two figures exist for the same loan in the same month: the interest the core banking system posted to the general ledger, and the interest the loan contract says should have been charged. The reconciliation puts them side by side, account by account, and names the reason for every difference. Open the sidebar, expand <b>EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition</b> and choose <b>GL Reconciliation</b>.</p>'
                . '<h4>The formula</h4>'
                . '<p>The expected interest for a month is the <b>outstanding balance at the end of the month before, times the annual interest rate, times the number of days in the calendar month, divided by 365</b>. This is not a theory about how the core system behaves. It is the calculation that reproduces the bank\'s own postings to the tambala, and it was proven on MAIIC\'s loan books and general ledger before it was built.</p>'
                . '<p>Three rules go with it:</p>'
                . '<ul>'
                . '<li><b>The month the money is paid out.</b> Interest runs from the disbursement day itself to the end of the month. Money paid out on 8 August is charged 24 days of August, not 23 and not a whole month.</li>'
                . '<li><b>A moratorium that defers interest as well as capital.</b> The interest is added to the balance every month, so the next month opens on a balance higher by exactly the interest charged.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Never the annual rate divided by twelve.</b> A twelfth of the year is not a month. In February a twelfth overstates the charge by between 8 and 9 percent, and no reconciliation survives that.</li>'
                . '</ul>'
                . '<h4>Where each input comes from</h4>'
                . '<p>The opening balance is the outstanding balance on the previous month\'s Loan Book Report. The rate is the one the loan book carries for the month being charged; where it carries none, the contract master is used, and the screen says which of the two was used. The day count and the band inside which a difference is treated as agreement are settings in the Governance Centre (<b>day count</b> and <b>reconciliation tolerance</b>), read for the month being reconciled. A change to either applies from its effective date forward, so a month already reported keeps the basis it was calculated on.</p>'
                . '<p>Where an input is missing the engine produces no figure at all. It never fills the gap with an assumption: the row says what is missing instead.</p>'
                . '<h4>The cause on each row, and what to do about it</h4>'
                . '<ul>'
                . '<li><b>Agrees.</b> The posting and the contract are inside the governed band. Nothing to do.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Late disbursement.</b> The loan was paid out during the month, so only part of the month is charged. Check the disbursement date on the contract against the first day the ledger accrued on; if the ledger started a day later, the disbursement date we hold may be wrong.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Catch-up posting.</b> The ledger posted nothing for one or more earlier months and then posted them all at once. The row names the months it covers. Read the posting as covering those months together; do not judge any single one of them on its own.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Mid-month tranche.</b> More money was drawn during the month, so the balance at the month end is not the balance the whole month was charged on. This is the case that needs the per-drawdown extract: the date and amount of each drawdown, so the days can be counted properly.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Rate mismatch.</b> The posting works out at a rate the loan book does not carry but another record does, usually the contract master or the offer letter. Ask MAIIC which rate the core system holds for the account, and whether it was repriced.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Nothing posted.</b> The loan book shows the account live with a balance and the ledger has no interest for it at all. Ask for the missing posting, or for the reason the account was not accrued: a loan in litigation or on a suspense account is a valid answer, and it belongs in the audit file.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Data gap.</b> Something the calculation needs is missing, so there is no expected figure. The row names it: no loan book for the month before, no rate on record, no disbursement date. Load the missing month or ask for the missing field, then look again.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Unexplained.</b> None of the causes above accounts for the difference. The row shows the rate the posting implies so the account can be investigated. These rows are reported, never absorbed into a total, and each one should be cleared or explained in writing before the period is closed.</li>'
                . '</ul>'
                . '<h4>The bridge at the top of the screen</h4>'
                . '<p>The bridge walks from what the ledger posted to the interest the engine calculated at the effective interest rate, in four steps that add up exactly. The <b>base effect</b> is the ledger against the contract, which is the total of the differences the causes explain. The <b>carrying amount effect</b> is the same charge worked out on the balance the engine amortises rather than the balance the loan book reports. The <b>rate effect</b> is the difference between accruing at the effective interest rate and charging the contractual rate on the same balance, which is where the fees that are part of the yield show up. The <b>impairment effect</b> is a Stage 3 loan accruing on the amount net of its loss allowance, which is correct under IFRS 9 and not an error. Anything the four cannot account for is shown as unexplained rather than hidden.</p>'
                . '<h4>The downloads</h4>'
                . '<p><b>Download Excel</b> and <b>Download PDF</b> give the whole month: a header block stating the formula, the day count and the band in force, one row per account with its opening balance, rate, days, expected interest, posted interest, difference and cause, and a table saying what each cause means. Both need the EIR export permission, and every download is recorded in the audit trail with the settings it was produced under.</p>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open GL Reconciliation from the EIR & Revenue Recognition group in the sidebar.',
                'Choose the reporting period and, if you want a narrower view, the portfolio.',
                'Read the four cards: interest posted in the ledger, contractual interest expected, the difference to explain, and how many rows agree.',
                'Work down the facilities table from the largest difference. The Cause column names the reason and the line under it explains that row in particular.',
                'Clear every row whose cause asks something of you: a missing month, a missing rate, a missing drawdown date, a posting the ledger never made.',
                'Investigate anything marked Unexplained and write down the conclusion before the period is closed.',
                'Press Download Excel or Download PDF to keep the month, with the settings it was worked out under, for the audit file.',
            ],
            'routes' => ['eir-reconciliation.index'],
        ],
    ],
];
